WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.080
The first film ended on a problem.

2
00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:07.880
Both pathways run downhill, so one liver cell could run them both at once.

3
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.740
Now meet two near-twin molecules.

4
00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:16.060
Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate is a step on the road to glucose.

5
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.720
Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate is on neither road.

6
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.440
So why does the liver make it?

7
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:28.040
First, the stakes.

8
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.680
At each one-way step, the two pathways use opposing enzymes, a pair.

9
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.420
Run both at full speed, and glucose is broken down and rebuilt for nothing.

10
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.400
How many high-energy phosphates does one turn waste?

11
00:00:49.433 --> 00:00:55.753
Four. Rebuilding spends six, and breaking down returns only two.

12
00:00:55.753 --> 00:01:01.113
One is lost at the glucose pair, one at the middle pair, and two at the pyruvate

13
00:01:01.113 --> 00:01:03.893
pair. All of it ends as heat.

14
00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:07.073
Now zoom in on the middle pair.

15
00:01:08.413 --> 00:01:13.133
PFK-1 puts a phosphate on, making fructose 1, 6 -bisphosphate.

16
00:01:14.253 --> 00:01:16.813
FBP has one cuts that phosphate back off.

17
00:01:18.153 --> 00:01:20.293
Both enzymes work in the same liver cell.

18
00:01:21.317 --> 00:01:23.417
This is where the twin comes in.

19
00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:30.777
Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate speeds up PFK-1 and slows FBPase-1.

20
00:01:31.957 --> 00:01:33.857
One molecule, opposite effects.

21
00:01:35.157 --> 00:01:36.597
One, six is the pathway.

22
00:01:37.857 --> 00:01:39.577
Two, six is the regulator.

23
00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:42.507
So here is the one idea.

24
00:01:43.747 --> 00:01:47.627
Both pathways run downhill, so energy cannot choose the direction.

25
00:01:48.927 --> 00:01:50.607
The cell chooses by regulation.

26
00:01:51.987 --> 00:01:55.167
It turns one enzyme of a pair up and its opposite down.

27
00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:58.550
Who sets the level of that regulator?

28
00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:01.570
A single protein with two halves.

29
00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:06.290
Its kinase half makes fructose-2,6-bisphosphate.

30
00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:09.470
Its phosphatase half destroys it.

31
00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:12.290
Whichever half wins sets the level.

32
00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:17.473
After a meal, insulin keeps this protein free of an added phosphate.

33
00:02:18.733 --> 00:02:22.833
The kinase half wins, and fructose-2,6-bisphosphate rises.

34
00:02:24.493 --> 00:02:28.993
PFK-1 speeds up, FBPase-1 slows, and glycolysis runs.

35
00:02:30.117 --> 00:02:32.377
In a fast, glucagon takes over.

36
00:02:33.637 --> 00:02:37.217
Working through PKA, it adds a phosphate to the same protein.

37
00:02:38.577 --> 00:02:40.077
Now the phosphatase half wins.

38
00:02:41.577 --> 00:02:46.117
Fructose-2,6-bisphosphate is destroyed, and FBPase-1 runs.

39
00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:51.090
The pyruvate pair and the glucose pair have controls of their own.

40
00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:56.810
Together, in a healthy liver cell, one direction dominates, and the waste stays

41
00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:57.810
small.

42
00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.600
So direction is not set by which enzymes are present.

43
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:04.420
Both sides of the pair are there.

44
00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:07.360
Downhill made both roads possible.

45
00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.540
Regulation decides which one carries the traffic.
